WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.997
(audience cheers)

00:00:01.997 --> 00:00:04.978
- Wow, thank you so much!

00:00:04.978 --> 00:00:06.963
- I don't think I've ever met anyone

00:00:06.963 --> 00:00:08.850
with such an interesting career.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.060
Like, you were in boxing
for like 15 years, right?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.893
- Correct.

00:00:12.893 --> 00:00:14.282
- Yeah, and first of
all, welcome to the show.

00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:16.170
- Thank you so much.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.820
- But also then, like, what...

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.590
Like, how did this start?

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.670
How did you start boxing?

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.840
Why was that a thing for you?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.610
- Well, you know, I'm one of five children

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.290
and I have two older brothers.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.000
I know we were talking
about siblings earlier,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.580
and I've always been
really sporty as a kid,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.350
basketball, softball.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:32.490
But my mom was really girly.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.590
She wanted me to wear dresses
and I'm, like, in trees

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.170
and I just wanted to fight
the boys in the neighborhood.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.180
But boxing was something that nobody did

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.260
in my family or anything.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.330
And I just really liked
this whole aspect of boxing.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.380
It kind of found me.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.900
And I needed somewhat of an outlet

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.040
going through some things as a kid.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.180
- Yeah.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.920
- I got my butt whooped, my first fight,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.230
my first amateur fight, and
it was one of those things,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.240
do I say this isn't for me,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.670
or I wanted to see how
does that not happen again?

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.570
So I just honed in on it

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.250
and wanted to just
become the best I could.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.900
- Yeah, you're badass.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:01.733
- Thank you.
(audience cheers)

00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.697
- Yeah.

00:01:03.697 --> 00:01:05.770
How did it go from boxing to acting?

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.160
Like, did you always kind
of have a dream for that,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.120
like, secretly?

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.060
- You know, I've been asking
myself this question a lot.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.110
Like, how did this happen?

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.000
I really don't know.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.720
It just kind of happened, I mean...

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.250
- Those are the best things,
though, that you don't...

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.900
It just organically happens in life.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.390
- Yeah, I mean, I'm really artistic.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.430
You know, I played the violin
when I was nine years old,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.080
come from a musically inclined family,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.297
and it was, like, something
that I wasn't like,

00:01:28.297 --> 00:01:29.970
"I wanna grow up and I
wanna be in Hollywood."

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:30.990
It was like, "That'd be kind of cool.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.340
I want to kind of try it one day."

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.380
But I did have this, like,
thing that I used to do

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.340
when my mom, when she'd come home,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.350
I'd, like, play around with her, like,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.700
dresses and stuff like that

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.320
and come up with these characters.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.153
- Yeah.

00:01:41.153 --> 00:01:41.986
- And like, I had these
specific characters.

00:01:41.986 --> 00:01:43.560
I would just entertain
my family all the time.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.480
But I'm like, you know, maybe
one day it will find me.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.680
If it's meant for me, it'll find me.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.810
And then it found me.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.070
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- It found me.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.920
- I know, it found you and
you're incredible at both things.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.880
It's such a...

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.680
It's just a random career, right?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.970
That, like, not many people have.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:57.930
That's such a blessing.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.670
I think it's cool to
stand out in that way.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.600
- Absolutely.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.130
Your mom gave you a nickname, right?

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.440
- So, my Native American
name is Mequinonoag,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.140
which means "many feathers, many talents."

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:07.973
- Love!

00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:09.930
- I guess she kind of
saw what I was gonna do.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.790
- Yeah, I was gonna say, wow.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.490
Nostradamus, I was like,
"She saw that coming."

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.260
- Yeah, so, it was actually quite an honor

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.090
'cause she saw a lot of things in me

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.430
I didn't see in myself as a kid,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.080
and it took a while for me
to kind of get comfortable

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.200
where I am now, so I embrace
that name and just...

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.420
I stick to it, making sure
I get the best out of myself

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.650
no matter what it is, you know?

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.360
And I have many talents to...

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.250
And many gifts, too, to
share with the world.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.020
- And who knows what else as well?

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.310
- [Kali] I don't know.
- Yeah.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.410
How did this role, like,
how did it find you?

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.243
How did you find it?

00:02:38.243 --> 00:02:39.300
How did you land in the show?

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.330
- So, "Catch The Fair One"
was my first ever time acting

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.860
and I actually helped write the film

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.230
and they were looking for
somebody to fill the role

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.990
of Navarro and Issa Lopez
and her casting director

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.460
got ahold of "Catch The Fair One".

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.880
She just wanted to basically
kind of know how I started,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.470
what my process was.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.730
She also, Issa involves murdered
women in a lot of her work

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:01.200
because she's from Mexico and Mexico City,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.090
so there's a lot of that
epidemic going around.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.610
So, we kind of collaborated
and just had a conversation

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.990
on what we did with "Catch The Fair One"

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.550
and what she does in her work.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:09.990
And I auditioned for it,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.437
and it was another thing where I was like,

00:03:11.437 --> 00:03:13.680
"If it's meant for me, it'll happen."

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.760
I actually wrote...

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.470
Funny story, I wrote "I will get the part

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.720
of Evangeline Navarro in 'True
Detective: Night Country'"

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.820
over and over in my notebook,
and then I got the call!

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:21.653
- Project that.

00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:23.392
- [Kali] Yes, man.
(audience applauds)

00:03:23.392 --> 00:03:24.225
- [Kelly] Exactly.

00:03:24.225 --> 00:03:25.077
- [Kali] Yes, absolutely.

00:03:25.077 --> 00:03:25.923
- Oh my gosh.

00:03:25.923 --> 00:03:29.070
Well, how has being on the
show helped you highlight

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.510
what's happening with indigenous women?

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.120
Because I had no idea
until I saw this movie,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.811
I can't remember the name,
but Jeremy Renner was in it.

00:03:35.811 --> 00:03:37.147
- "Wind River".

00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:37.980
"Wind River".

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:38.813
- "Wind River".

00:03:38.813 --> 00:03:40.710
I had no idea, and it broke my heart.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.427
At the end of the movie, they put like,

00:03:42.427 --> 00:03:43.860
"This really is happening."

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.180
And I was like, "Wait, what?"

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:46.290
And it's such a...

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.020
A lot of people don't know,
they're just disappearing.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:49.853
- Yes.

00:03:49.853 --> 00:03:50.686
- These women are disappearing.

00:03:50.686 --> 00:03:53.640
Like, how do you feel like
your success has honestly

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.680
had an impact on the community, right?

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:56.790
- I would like to think so.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.940
I mean, with my boxing
career, I did my best

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.320
to just bring awareness to missing

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.650
and murdered indigenous people or MMIW.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.560
It's one of those things
that help highlight

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.450
exactly what's happening
in indigenous communities.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.700
It's not just our women,
it's our men, our women,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.370
our boys, our elders and our
people are getting targeted.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.660
So, it's one of those things.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.700
Even with these earrings
with the red hand print,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.430
anytime you see these red
hand prints on anything,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.350
it's basically, you'll
see a red hand print

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.070
over their mouths saying
they're silencing on our voices.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.630
So, with my boxing career,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.860
I had an audience that didn't know,

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.210
just like you didn't know about it.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.790
So, I would just speak about
it in front of audiences

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.380
that don't know about
it, to spread the word,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.720
to spread awareness, to stop
this violence against women.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.610
So, to be involved in any kind of projects

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.800
or anything to be able to talk about it.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.633
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:42.750
- You know, we're just
taught to fight for those

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:43.920
who can't fight, speak
for those who can't speak.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:44.850
- You have to talk about it.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:45.900
People didn't even know.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.180
Like, I was sitting there,
and I felt, like foolish,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.110
and I was ashamed.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.700
I was watching it and I
was like, "Wait, what?"

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.290
I had no idea, you know?

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:53.628
- It happens, it doesn't happen to just-

00:04:53.628 --> 00:04:54.750
- [Kelly] But that's
what art is for, right?

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.583
- Exactly.

00:04:55.583 --> 00:04:58.530
It's not just one area, it's
not just one area of America.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.900
It's in Canada, it's any indigenous people

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.130
in anywhere in the world, you'll see

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.900
that we're targeted as a people,

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.670
but especially here in North America.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.500
And it happens right next door.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.333
- Yeah.

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:10.023
So I'm glad you're using
your spotlight for that.

00:05:10.023 --> 00:05:11.130
- Absolutely.
- It's important.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.457
Well, everybody be sure to check out

00:05:12.457 --> 00:05:14.853
"True Detective: Night Country" on Max.

